description:
hire-attestors/src/oidc.rs — claim_from_jwt() at line 75-79 encodes 'is the token expired' into the assurance level:

    let assurance = if exp > now_unix() { Iaa2 } else { Iaa1 };

and freshness() at line 197 then decodes it back out:

    if c.assurance >= IdentityAssurance::Iaa2 { Fresh } else { Stale(None) }

What goes wrong: `exp`, the actual value being tested, is read at line 66 and immediately discarded, so the staleness check is performed against a lossy proxy. The coupling is invisible from either end — a reader at line 197 sees an assurance comparison and has no reason to know it means 'not expired', and anyone who later adds a third assurance tier, or makes an unexpired token Iaa1 for some other reason (a token from a weaker issuer, say), silently breaks the freshness check with no compiler error and no failing test. The mapping is also asserted nowhere.

Rewrite: keep the value instead of re-deriving it. Have the scan carry `exp` alongside the claim — scan_token_caches() already returns a tuple (oidc.rs:96, `Vec<(Claim, String)>`), so make it `Vec<(Claim, String, u64)>` or a small named struct — and have freshness() compare `exp > now_unix()` directly. The assurance level then means only what its name says.
